            {/* 42. No Liability Boot Failure */}
            <section id="no-liability-boot-failure" className="space-y-4">
              <h2 className="text-lg font-semibold text-white">42. No Liability for Boot Failure</h2>
              <div className="text-[14px] leading-[1.9] text-white/55 font-light space-y-4">
                <p>YOU HEREBY ACKNOWLEDGE AND AGREE THat Impulse SHALL NOT BE LIABLE UNDER ANY CIRCUMSTANCES, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO NEGLIGENCE, GROSS NEGLIGENCE, WILLFUL MISCONDUCT, STRICT LIABILITY, OR ANY OTHER LEGAL THEORY, FOR ANY BOOT FAILURE, FAILURE TO POST (POWER-ON SELF-TEST), OPERATING SYSTEM LOAD FAILURE, BOOT LOOP, INABILITY TO ACCESS THE OPERATING SYSTEM, OR ANY OTHER FORM OF STARTUP OR BOOT-RELATED FAILURE ARISING FROM OR RELATING TO THE SOFTWARE OR SERVICES.</p>
                <p>You acknowledge that system modifications, driver changes, registry edits, BIOS configuration changes, and other optimization activities can affect the system boot process and may cause temporary or permanent boot failure. Boot failure is a known and inherent risk of system modification and cannot be entirely eliminated, even with careful and conservative practices.</p>
                <p>Boot failure can manifest in various forms, including but not limited to: system fails to power on at all; system powers on but displays no video output (black screen); system displays POST error codes or beep codes; system enters a boot loop (repeatedly restarts); operating system fails to load past the boot logo or loading screen; operating system loads but immediately crashes or restarts; operating system loads into safe mode or automatic repair mode; operating system loads but critical drivers or services fail to start; and system displays "boot device not found" or similar error messages.</p>
                <p>Impulse technicians take reasonable precautions to minimize the risk of boot failure, including but not limited to: creating System Restore Points before modifications; documenting all changes made during the session; providing guidance on recovery procedures in the event of boot failure; and avoiding known high-risk modifications when safer alternatives are available. However, despite all reasonable precautions, boot failure can still occur.</p>
                <p>In the event of boot failure, Impulse may, at its sole discretion and as a courtesy, provide guidance on troubleshooting and recovery procedures. However, Impulse shall have no obligation to resolve boot failures, and any assistance provided shall not create liability on the part of Impulse. You acknowledge that some boot failures may require operating system reinstallation, system recovery from backup, or hardware troubleshooting that Impulse is not obligated to provide.</p>
                <p>You are solely responsible for maintaining bootable recovery media, system image backups, and recovery tools to address any boot failure that may occur. Impulse strongly recommends that you create a bootable USB recovery drive and a full system image backup before any Service session.</p>
              </div>
            </section>

